WEBVTT

00:02.070 --> 00:03.280
California.

00:03.509 --> 00:07.040
A great state to visit and
a wonderful place to live.

00:07.219 --> 00:10.160
Great weather, opportunity, and beautiful

00:10.161 --> 00:13.440
landscapes, but it faces
a growing challenge.

00:13.620 --> 00:17.760
Wildfires return every
year, and climate change

00:17.761 --> 00:22.080
intensifies the hot, dry
conditions that help them spread.

00:22.385 --> 00:26.521
When fire approaches,
families face urgent decisions.

00:26.610 --> 00:32.720
Homes, businesses, and entire communities
can be threatened in a matter of hours.

00:32.999 --> 00:38.800
Brave firefighters and emergency responders
work tirelessly to protect lives.

00:38.980 --> 00:42.521
Scientists and engineers
innovate to support their efforts.

00:42.679 --> 00:45.840
One of those innovations
is FireWatch Sentinel.

00:46.069 --> 00:48.640
Connecting environmental
sensing with practical

00:48.641 --> 00:53.120
information, it helps people watch
the signs and protect what matters.

00:53.250 --> 00:56.720
It begins with a compact
sensor node, combining

00:56.721 --> 01:02.320
observations of the tree, soil, and
surrounding air in one connected system.

01:02.325 --> 01:05.360
Inside a healthy tree,
water travels from the

01:05.361 --> 01:10.880
roots through tiny channels called xylem,
supplying the branches and leaves above.

01:11.109 --> 01:13.920
Drought puts that
water supply under stress.

01:14.149 --> 01:18.720
Gas bubbles can form in these
channels, interrupting water transport.

01:18.999 --> 01:20.960
This is called cavitation.

01:21.139 --> 01:24.240
Cavitation can produce
tiny acoustic emissions.

01:24.469 --> 01:27.680
FireWatch Sentinel captures tree vibrations

01:27.681 --> 01:32.560
through contact sensing, adding
another window into changing conditions.

01:32.839 --> 01:36.320
As drought stress builds, trees can weaken,

01:36.320 --> 01:40.521
vegetation can dry, and
dead material can accumulate.

01:40.609 --> 01:45.200
Understanding these changes helps us
see the landscape with greater awareness.

01:45.429 --> 01:50.800
Temperature, humidity, and soil
moisture add essential context.

01:50.979 --> 01:53.680
Together, these
measurements reveal conditions

01:53.681 --> 01:56.400
around each node,
connecting what is happening in

01:56.401 --> 01:58.960
the air with what is
happening below ground.

01:59.189 --> 02:01.840
Airborne particulate
monitoring adds another

02:01.841 --> 02:06.400
layer of information, tracking
particles in the surrounding air.

02:06.729 --> 02:12.000
It brings air quality into the same picture
as the other environmental readings.

02:12.230 --> 02:17.520
A camera adds visual context, giving
people a view of the monitored area.

02:17.750 --> 02:20.800
Measurements and images
work together, making the

02:20.801 --> 02:23.360
information easier to
understand and review.

02:23.465 --> 02:26.960
The node sends its
information outward, linking

02:26.961 --> 02:30.521
observations in the field with
the people who need them.

02:30.600 --> 02:34.320
From one tree, we begin
to see the wider landscape.

02:34.500 --> 02:40.000
Across multiple locations, a network of
sensor nodes captures local differences.

02:40.230 --> 02:43.840
A shaded woodland, an
exposed hillside, and an

02:43.841 --> 02:47.840
area near a neighborhood
can each tell a different story.

02:48.120 --> 02:52.400
The FireWatch Sentinel dashboard
brings those observations together.

02:52.655 --> 02:56.240
Clear readings, trend
graphs, and camera views

02:56.241 --> 02:59.120
give users a shared place
to examine conditions

02:59.390 --> 03:02.880
and understand what each node is reporting.

03:03.135 --> 03:06.080
Users can move between nodes, compare local

03:06.081 --> 03:09.440
readings, and review the
associated camera views.

03:09.770 --> 03:12.480
Instead of looking at
isolated measurements, they

03:12.481 --> 03:15.680
can build a clearer picture
across the monitored area.

03:15.960 --> 03:19.440
The dashboard supports
a focused review, examine

03:19.600 --> 03:23.440
the readings, check the visual context, and

03:23.441 --> 03:26.640
identify where a closer
look would be useful.

03:26.820 --> 03:30.240
Information becomes a
starting point for informed action.

03:30.370 --> 03:34.720
Over time, those observations
build a valuable record.

03:34.850 --> 03:38.800
Agencies, planners, and
insurance companies can

03:38.801 --> 03:42.000
use environmental data
to support assessment and

03:42.001 --> 03:46.800
planning, while local teams use
current information to guide field checks.

03:47.080 --> 03:49.760
FireWatch Sentinel is doing its part for

03:49.935 --> 03:52.800
California, connecting
environmental sensing,

03:52.801 --> 03:56.800
useful information, and the people
who care for our communities.

03:56.930 --> 03:58.640
Watch the signs.

03:58.820 --> 04:00.500
Protect what matters.
